
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

## Running the tests

Tests run natively on your machine (not on the ESP32) via the `native`
environment defined in [platformio.ini](../platformio.ini). This environment
fakes the Arduino core with ArduinoFake, so no board or serial connection
is required.

### Using the PlatformIO CLI

From the project root:

```powershell
# Run all test suites
pio test -e native

# Run a single suite, e.g. test_motorHandler
pio test -e native -f test_motorHandler

# Run with verbose output
pio test -e native -v
```

If `pio` isn't on your PATH, use the full path instead, e.g.
`C:\Users\<you>\.platformio\penv\Scripts\platformio.exe test -e native`.

### Using the VS Code PlatformIO extension

Open the PlatformIO sidebar (ant icon) > **PROJECT TASKS** > **native** >
**Advanced** > **Test**, or click the checkmark icon in the PlatformIO
status bar at the bottom of the window.

### Notes

- Do not compile test files directly with `g++`/a generic C/C++ task — they
  depend on Unity, ArduinoFake, and the stubs in [test/stubs](stubs), which
  PlatformIO wires up automatically for the `native` environment.
- Each test suite includes the `src/` files it needs directly (see
  `test_build_src = false` in [platformio.ini](../platformio.ini)) and owns
  its own Unity `main()`, so suites can also be run independently.
